\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Temporal gauge and the residual spatial action}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Use the [spatial graph and link space](fdip-007Z)
#{X=G^{E_s}} and #{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}}=L^2(X,dU;\mathbb C)}.
On a finite open time interval with slices #{0,\ldots,N}, the
[temporal link](fdip-0083) #{h_{j,v}} transports from #{j+1} to
#{j} at spatial vertex #{v}. Allow independent gauge changes at
all slices. For slice gauge changes #{g_j\in K},
where #{K=G^{V_s}}, it transforms as
##{h_{j,v}\longmapsto
 g_{j,v}h_{j,v}g_{j+1,v}^{-1}.}
\newvocab{Temporal gauge} means #{h_{j,v}=1} on these temporal
edges. Choose #{g_{0,v}} and recursively set
#{g_{j+1,v}=g_{j,v}h_{j,v}} to reach this gauge.}

\p{A transformation preserving temporal gauge satisfies
#{g_{j+1,v}=g_{j,v}}. Its remaining freedom is one spatial gauge
transformation #{g\in K}, independent of time. This is the
\newvocab{residual gauge action}:
##{(g\cdot U)_e=g_{t(e)}U_eg_{s(e)}^{-1},\qquad
 (R_gf)(U)=f(g^{-1}\cdot U).}
These are the same unitary operators used in the transfer
construction, now interpreted on a time slice.}

\p{On a periodic time circle, the closure #{g_{N,v}=g_{0,v}}
changes the conclusion. The ordered temporal holonomy is
##{H_v=h_{0,v}h_{1,v}\cdots h_{N-1,v}.}
The recursion closes exactly when #{H_v=1}. In general one can
set the first #{N-1} temporal links to one, but the last becomes
#{g_{0,v}H_vg_{0,v}^{-1}}. Thus an arbitrary periodic field does
not admit a gauge with every temporal link equal to one. The
[projected periodic trace](fdip-0083) keeps its temporal averaging
and does not require such a gauge.}

\p{Kogut and Susskind's 1975 Hamiltonian formulation of Wilson's
lattice gauge theories \citek{kogut1975hamiltonian} provides
historical context for this subject. The finite residual
action above follows directly from the displayed transformation
law; the state constraints below are established by averaging and
representation theory.}
